WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.299
The internet is a buzz about Sony's new Spider-Man
adjacent film "Madame Web," but maybe not

00:00:05.299 --> 00:00:06.840
in the way the studio might hope.

00:00:07.432 --> 00:00:09.147
Is it time for a new "Morbin' time?"

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:14.009
Sony's Spider-Man Universe—that still doesn't
have a Spider-Man—is developing a less-than-stellar

00:00:14.009 --> 00:00:15.440
reputation for itself.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.730
While superhero movie trailers usually command
attention and anticipation, that isn't happening

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:23.140
with its latest offering — the trailer for
"Madame Web," starring Dakota Johnson, Adam

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.340
Scott, and Sydney Sweeney.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.010
The film isn't even out yet, and it's already
earning the collective ire of the internet

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:31.570
and may have already been memed more than
"Morbius."

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:35.300
People can have differing opinions, but the
collective consensus online seems to be that

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.061
"Madame Web" looks ... not great.

00:00:38.061 --> 00:00:42.309
From poor dialogue to needlessly convoluted
exposition, "Madame Web" has plenty of people

00:00:42.309 --> 00:00:44.489
loving to hate it on the world wide web.

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:48.370
Many on X, formerly known as Twitter, were
quick to point out how the costumes and acting

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:52.200
are reminiscent of what one might see on a
CW superhero show.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.920
Others are already predicting very low box
office numbers, how low?

00:00:56.554 --> 00:00:57.554
"Seven bucks!?"

00:00:57.554 --> 00:01:01.190
One of the best things to come out of "Morbius,"
or maybe the only good thing depending on

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:05.040
who you ask, was the internet coining the
phrase, "It's morbin' time!"

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.330
Despite not being in the movie, it caught
on quickly and became a meme because it sounds

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:11.409
like the kind of cringey dialogue a movie
like "Morbius" would have.

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:15.301
"Madame Web" falls into that camp, and people
have no qualms over coming up with their own

00:01:15.301 --> 00:01:17.049
fake dialogue for the film.

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:20.670
One can spot a trend in the YouTube comment
section of the "Madame Web" trailer.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.430
People mention loving a part of the trailer
that isn't there.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:24.909
For example, one commenter wrote,

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:29.939
"I love that part of the trailer when she
said 'It's webbing time' I cried [tears] of joy."

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:32.730
Others got a bit more obscure with their jokes,
with one user writing,

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:36.400
"I love the part when she says 'You know,
I'm something of a spidergirl myself' and

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:37.780
proceeded to web.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:39.720
Truly a movie of all time."

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:43.774
This trend is repeated ad nauseam under the
video, but the common sentiment is clear:

00:01:43.774 --> 00:01:45.599
No one really respects this movie.

00:01:45.599 --> 00:01:49.920
Even BossLogic, who makes custom posters and
artwork for superhero movies and is usually

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:51.960
pretty complimentary, wrote on X,

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:55.710
"So looking forward to it but #MadameWeb should
have just released a teaser for now, till

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.649
they had a trailer that will blow us away."

00:01:57.649 --> 00:02:01.890
But all the fake dialogue in the world couldn't
hold a candle to an actual line in the trailer

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.729
that's taken on a life of its own.

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:07.810
One major complaint against the "Madame Web"
trailer is that, at only three minutes long,

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.830
it gives away much of the plot, including
the antagonist's backstory.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.910
At one point, Cassandra Web says,

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:17.800
"He was in the Amazon with my mom when she
was researching spiders right before she died."

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.700
With that line, the internet would never be
the same.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.819
The clunky line delivered with all the passion
of a 4th grader delivering a book report for

00:02:24.819 --> 00:02:28.050
a book they didn't read has inspired countless
memes and jokes.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:32.349
One user posted a still from "The Room" featuring
Tommy Wiseau and imagined that his line reading

00:02:32.349 --> 00:02:34.461
would be very similar to this famous scene,

00:02:34.461 --> 00:02:36.711
"I did not hit her, it's not true.

00:02:36.711 --> 00:02:37.500
It's bulls---.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.500
I did not hit her.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:39.709
I did not.

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:41.403
Oh, hi Mark."

00:02:41.477 --> 00:02:42.329
They wrote,

00:02:42.329 --> 00:02:43.329
"It's not true.

00:02:43.487 --> 00:02:44.487
It's bulls---.

00:02:44.487 --> 00:02:48.287
I did not go to the Amazon with your mom when
she was researching spiders just before she died.

00:02:48.329 --> 00:02:49.329
I did naaaht.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:51.209
Oh hi Madame Web."

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:54.069
What is it about this line that's captured
the internet's attention?

00:02:54.069 --> 00:02:56.440
Is it how it crams in so much lore in a single
sentence?

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:01.110
Honestly, taking out the superfluous detail
of "she was researching spiders" would probably

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:04.050
help it flow more smoothly, but what's done
is done.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.129
Who knows?

00:03:05.129 --> 00:03:08.360
Maybe more people will check out "Madame Web"
when it comes out in theaters on February

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:12.120
14, 2024 to see what other meme-worthy gems
could be hiding in it.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.349
Then again, people may just make up stuff,
and those who haven't seen the movie will

00:03:15.349 --> 00:03:16.753
simply think it's true.

